1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,063
A password inside a pipeline is a password waiting to leak.

2
00:00:04,613 --> 00:00:06,328
It leaks into the repository,

3
00:00:06,328 --> 00:00:07,452
into the build log,

4
00:00:07,452 --> 00:00:08,871
and onto a screen share.

5
00:00:08,871 --> 00:00:09,936
And once it leaks,

6
00:00:09,936 --> 00:00:11,887
you have to change it everywhere.

7
00:00:12,437 --> 00:00:15,620
Today you learn the Jenkins way: store a secret once,

8
00:00:15,620 --> 00:00:17,482
and let the pipeline borrow it,

9
00:00:17,482 --> 00:00:17,963
by name.

10
00:00:18,513 --> 00:00:20,018
You will see a real leak,

11
00:00:20,018 --> 00:00:21,163
with a real secret,

12
00:00:21,163 --> 00:00:22,186
in the build log.

13
00:00:22,186 --> 00:00:23,451
Then you will fix it,

14
00:00:23,451 --> 00:00:25,318
and watch the secret disappear.

15
00:00:26,868 --> 00:00:28,297
Here is what to expect.

16
00:00:28,847 --> 00:00:31,087
One: the four places where secrets leak.

17
00:00:31,637 --> 00:00:36,331
Two: how the Jenkins credentials store works, and the kinds of credential it holds.

18
00:00:36,881 --> 00:00:40,005
Three: a live demo in the real Jenkins screens.

19
00:00:40,005 --> 00:00:41,268
We add credentials,

20
00:00:41,268 --> 00:00:42,597
leak one on purpose,

21
00:00:42,597 --> 00:00:43,062
fix it,

22
00:00:43,062 --> 00:00:44,923
then use two kinds together.

23
00:00:45,473 --> 00:00:49,591
Four: why masking is not a vault, and the rules that keep a team safe.

24
00:00:50,141 --> 00:00:53,063
This is objective five of the Jenkins domain.

25
00:00:55,113 --> 00:00:56,884
Four places where secrets leak.

26
00:00:57,434 --> 00:00:58,776
One: the Jenkinsfile.

27
00:00:58,776 --> 00:00:59,752
It lives in git,

28
00:00:59,752 --> 00:01:03,108
so a password typed there stays in the history forever.

29
00:01:03,658 --> 00:01:05,115
Two: the console output.

30
00:01:05,115 --> 00:01:08,998
Any command that prints the secret writes it into the build log,

31
00:01:08,998 --> 00:01:11,061
for everyone who can read the job.

32
00:01:11,611 --> 00:01:13,611
Three: the job configuration.

33
00:01:13,611 --> 00:01:18,438
A secret pasted into a build step is plain text on the configure page.

34
00:01:18,988 --> 00:01:24,982
Four: environment dumps. A debug line that prints every variable also prints every secret.

35
00:01:25,532 --> 00:01:29,313
The cure is the same for all four: keep the secret out of the code,

36
00:01:29,313 --> 00:01:30,780
and borrow it at run time.

37
00:01:32,330 --> 00:01:33,994
How the credentials store works.

38
00:01:34,544 --> 00:01:39,408
You add a credential once, in the credentials store. Jenkins encrypts it on disk.

39
00:01:39,958 --> 00:01:41,712
Every credential has an ID.

40
00:01:41,712 --> 00:01:42,777
That is its name,

41
00:01:42,777 --> 00:01:45,910
and it is the only thing a pipeline ever mentions.

42
00:01:46,460 --> 00:01:48,810
The pipeline says: with credentials,

43
00:01:48,810 --> 00:01:49,397
this ID.

44
00:01:49,397 --> 00:01:52,138
Jenkins hands the secret to that one step,

45
00:01:52,138 --> 00:01:53,052
as a variable.

46
00:01:53,602 --> 00:01:55,954
And if the secret appears in the log,

47
00:01:55,954 --> 00:01:58,242
Jenkins replaces it with four stars.

48
00:01:58,242 --> 00:01:59,703
That is called masking.

49
00:02:00,253 --> 00:02:03,801
Credentials come in kinds: secret text for a token,

50
00:02:03,801 --> 00:02:05,401
username with password,

51
00:02:05,401 --> 00:02:06,305
an SSH key,

52
00:02:06,305 --> 00:02:07,279
a secret file,

53
00:02:07,279 --> 00:02:08,531
and a certificate.

54
00:02:10,081 --> 00:02:15,137
A real Jenkins, again. I sign in as admin. We start with the credentials store.

55
00:02:23,687 --> 00:02:24,761
Manage Jenkins,

56
00:02:24,761 --> 00:02:25,978
then Credentials.

57
00:02:25,978 --> 00:02:27,338
This page is empty.

58
00:02:27,338 --> 00:02:29,915
Stores scoped to Jenkins: one store,

59
00:02:29,915 --> 00:02:30,846
named System,

60
00:02:30,846 --> 00:02:32,492
with the Global domain.

61
00:02:32,492 --> 00:02:34,354
That is where we add ours.

62
00:02:34,904 --> 00:02:35,860
Click System,

63
00:02:35,860 --> 00:02:36,743
then Global.

64
00:02:36,743 --> 00:02:38,289
Then Add Credentials.

65
00:02:38,289 --> 00:02:41,011
Jenkins shows the kinds it can store.

66
00:02:41,011 --> 00:02:42,704
Username with password,

67
00:02:42,704 --> 00:02:43,440
SSH key,

68
00:02:43,440 --> 00:02:44,323
secret file,

69
00:02:44,323 --> 00:02:45,206
secret text,

70
00:02:45,206 --> 00:02:46,530
and a certificate.

71
00:02:47,080 --> 00:02:48,842
Our first one is a token,

72
00:02:48,842 --> 00:02:50,392
so I pick Secret text,

73
00:02:50,392 --> 00:02:51,449
and click Next.

74
00:02:51,449 --> 00:02:52,787
Scope stays Global.

75
00:02:52,787 --> 00:02:54,971
The secret is the token itself.

76
00:02:54,971 --> 00:02:56,662
The ID is deploy-token,

77
00:02:56,662 --> 00:02:57,789
short and clear.

78
00:02:57,789 --> 00:02:59,903
A description helps your team.

79
00:02:59,903 --> 00:03:00,819
Click Create.

80
00:03:06,369 --> 00:03:08,865
Second one: Username with password.

81
00:03:08,865 --> 00:03:10,649
The username is deployer,

82
00:03:10,649 --> 00:03:13,074
the password goes in the next box,

83
00:03:13,074 --> 00:03:15,214
and the ID is registry-login.

84
00:03:15,214 --> 00:03:15,713
Create.

85
00:03:16,263 --> 00:03:17,366
Both are listed,

86
00:03:17,366 --> 00:03:19,020
with their ID and kind.

87
00:03:19,020 --> 00:03:22,053
Now the important part: I open deploy-token,

88
00:03:22,053 --> 00:03:23,225
and click Update.

89
00:03:23,225 --> 00:03:24,948
Look at the secret field.

90
00:03:24,948 --> 00:03:26,189
It says Concealed.

91
00:03:26,189 --> 00:03:27,844
Nobody can read it back,

92
00:03:27,844 --> 00:03:29,084
not even an admin.

93
00:03:29,634 --> 00:03:30,491
Now the demo.

94
00:03:30,491 --> 00:03:31,085
New Item,

95
00:03:31,085 --> 00:03:31,810
a Pipeline,

96
00:03:31,810 --> 00:03:33,062
named secrets-demo.

97
00:03:33,062 --> 00:03:33,458
First,

98
00:03:33,458 --> 00:03:34,380
the wrong way,

99
00:03:34,380 --> 00:03:36,292
the way many beginners start.

100
00:03:36,292 --> 00:03:39,786
The token is typed straight into the pipeline script,

101
00:03:39,786 --> 00:03:41,368
and the stage prints it.

102
00:03:51,918 --> 00:03:52,254
Save,

103
00:03:52,254 --> 00:03:53,195
and Build Now.

104
00:03:53,195 --> 00:03:54,877
Jenkins queues the build,

105
00:03:54,877 --> 00:03:55,549
starts it,

106
00:03:55,549 --> 00:03:56,827
and runs the stage.

107
00:03:56,827 --> 00:03:58,508
That takes a few seconds,

108
00:03:58,508 --> 00:04:01,064
and the stage simply prints the token,

109
00:04:01,064 --> 00:04:01,804
as written.

110
00:04:01,804 --> 00:04:03,149
Open Console Output.

111
00:04:03,149 --> 00:04:03,956
There it is.

112
00:04:03,956 --> 00:04:04,695
The secret,

113
00:04:04,695 --> 00:04:05,637
in clear text,

114
00:04:05,637 --> 00:04:06,780
in the build log.

115
00:04:06,780 --> 00:04:10,008
Anyone who can read this job can read the token.

116
00:04:10,558 --> 00:04:11,724
Now the right way.

117
00:04:11,724 --> 00:04:12,825
I open Configure,

118
00:04:12,825 --> 00:04:14,314
and rewrite the script.

119
00:04:14,314 --> 00:04:16,256
Watch the editor while I type.

120
00:04:16,256 --> 00:04:18,652
The line that held the token is gone.

121
00:04:18,652 --> 00:04:19,494
In its place,

122
00:04:19,494 --> 00:04:22,343
with credentials takes the ID deploy-token,

123
00:04:22,343 --> 00:04:25,127
and hands the step a variable called TOKEN.

124
00:04:25,127 --> 00:04:26,292
Inside that block,

125
00:04:26,292 --> 00:04:29,271
the same echo command now prints the variable.

126
00:04:29,271 --> 00:04:31,796
The pipeline never contains the secret,

127
00:04:31,796 --> 00:04:32,702
only its name.

128
00:04:33,252 --> 00:04:33,660
Build.

129
00:04:33,660 --> 00:04:35,357
Jenkins saves the script,

130
00:04:35,357 --> 00:04:36,918
and runs the new build.

131
00:04:36,918 --> 00:04:39,022
This takes a few seconds again.

132
00:04:39,022 --> 00:04:40,923
Now open the Console Output.

133
00:04:40,923 --> 00:04:42,280
The stage still ran,

134
00:04:42,280 --> 00:04:43,638
with the real token.

135
00:04:43,638 --> 00:04:45,606
But the log shows four stars.

136
00:04:45,606 --> 00:04:46,692
That is masking.

137
00:04:47,242 --> 00:04:48,684
Now both kinds together,

138
00:04:48,684 --> 00:04:50,065
plus a normal variable.

139
00:04:50,065 --> 00:04:53,128
The username and password come from registry-login.

140
00:04:53,128 --> 00:04:54,269
The plain variable,

141
00:04:54,269 --> 00:04:54,749
APP_ENV,

142
00:04:54,749 --> 00:04:55,590
is not secret,

143
00:04:55,590 --> 00:04:56,791
so it stays visible.

144
00:04:56,791 --> 00:04:57,151
Build,

145
00:04:57,151 --> 00:04:58,172
and read the log.

146
00:04:58,172 --> 00:04:59,914
Jenkins runs the build again.

147
00:04:59,914 --> 00:05:00,755
While it runs,

148
00:05:00,755 --> 00:05:03,217
notice what we print: the plain variable,

149
00:05:03,217 --> 00:05:04,238
the build number,

150
00:05:04,238 --> 00:05:05,019
the username,

151
00:05:05,019 --> 00:05:06,040
and the password.

152
00:05:06,040 --> 00:05:08,742
Jenkins only masks what it knows is a secret.

153
00:05:08,742 --> 00:05:09,943
Secrets are starred,

154
00:05:09,943 --> 00:05:11,925
and the plain values are visible.

155
00:05:24,475 --> 00:05:25,470
One last lesson,

156
00:05:25,470 --> 00:05:26,279
about limits.

157
00:05:26,279 --> 00:05:27,336
I open Configure,

158
00:05:27,336 --> 00:05:29,264
and add one line to the script.

159
00:05:29,264 --> 00:05:32,623
The line pipes the token through a command called rev,

160
00:05:32,623 --> 00:05:34,365
which prints text backwards.

161
00:05:34,365 --> 00:05:36,355
If a step transforms the secret,

162
00:05:36,355 --> 00:05:38,470
for example it prints it reversed,

163
00:05:38,470 --> 00:05:40,460
Jenkins no longer recognises it,

164
00:05:40,460 --> 00:05:42,326
and it shows up in clear text.

165
00:05:42,326 --> 00:05:42,762
I save,

166
00:05:42,762 --> 00:05:44,006
and build once more.

167
00:05:44,006 --> 00:05:45,001
Jenkins runs it,

168
00:05:45,001 --> 00:05:46,618
and we wait a few seconds.

169
00:05:46,618 --> 00:05:48,795
Masking only hides the exact value.

170
00:05:57,345 --> 00:05:59,067
So masking is a safety net,

171
00:05:59,067 --> 00:05:59,832
not a vault.

172
00:05:59,832 --> 00:06:03,212
The real protection is who may read and edit the job.

173
00:06:04,762 --> 00:06:07,237
Masking is not a vault. These are the rules.

174
00:06:07,787 --> 00:06:11,092
One: never type a secret into a Jenkinsfile,

175
00:06:11,092 --> 00:06:11,900
a job step,

176
00:06:11,900 --> 00:06:12,561
or a log.

177
00:06:12,561 --> 00:06:13,736
Use credentials,

178
00:06:13,736 --> 00:06:14,251
always.

179
00:06:14,801 --> 00:06:18,577
Two: never print a secret, or transform it, not even to debug.

180
00:06:19,127 --> 00:06:23,692
Three: give each credential the smallest scope, and only to the jobs that need it.

181
00:06:24,242 --> 00:06:29,127
Four: control who can edit pipelines. Whoever edits the script can reach the secret.

182
00:06:29,677 --> 00:06:30,485
Five: rotate.

183
00:06:30,485 --> 00:06:32,348
Change a secret on a schedule,

184
00:06:32,348 --> 00:06:34,336
and at once when someone leaves.

185
00:06:34,336 --> 00:06:36,013
You change it in one place.

186
00:06:37,563 --> 00:06:39,440
Five mistakes with credentials.

187
00:06:39,990 --> 00:06:44,366
One: a secret as a plain environment variable in the Jenkinsfile.

188
00:06:44,366 --> 00:06:45,228
It is in git,

189
00:06:45,228 --> 00:06:46,156
in clear text.

190
00:06:46,706 --> 00:06:50,994
Two: echo of a secret, to see if it works. It works, and it is in the log.

191
00:06:51,544 --> 00:06:56,984
Three: one global admin token for every job. When it leaks, everything is exposed.

192
00:06:57,534 --> 00:07:00,190
Four: a credential ID that does not match.

193
00:07:00,190 --> 00:07:03,218
The build fails with: could not find credentials.

194
00:07:03,218 --> 00:07:04,391
Check the spelling,

195
00:07:04,391 --> 00:07:05,256
and the scope.

196
00:07:05,806 --> 00:07:11,566
Five: never rotating. A secret that never changes is a secret that someone already has.

197
00:07:13,116 --> 00:07:15,911
Where to find the lab. Pause here, and write it down.

198
00:07:16,461 --> 00:07:17,925
One: the repository,

199
00:07:17,925 --> 00:07:22,245
git hub dot com slash it dash waves slash dev ops dash A I,

200
00:07:22,245 --> 00:07:24,589
in the folder academy slash lab.

201
00:07:25,139 --> 00:07:31,091
Two: the files, lab one fifty-eight dot S H, and shortcuts one fifty-eight dot S H.

202
00:07:31,641 --> 00:07:37,102
Three: install it, with sudo install, into slash user slash local slash S bin.

203
00:07:37,652 --> 00:07:39,456
Four: load the short names,

204
00:07:39,456 --> 00:07:42,528
with source shortcuts one fifty-eight dot S H.

205
00:07:42,528 --> 00:07:43,330
Then lab up,

206
00:07:43,330 --> 00:07:44,265
and lab files.

207
00:07:44,815 --> 00:07:49,636
Five: lab files writes the leaky and the safe pipeline scripts into a folder,

208
00:07:49,636 --> 00:07:50,575
ready to paste.

209
00:07:51,125 --> 00:07:54,661
Six: you need the Jenkins from lesson one fifty-five.

210
00:07:54,661 --> 00:07:57,931
Lab down removes the lab credentials and the job.

211
00:07:58,481 --> 00:08:00,123
The link is in the description.

212
00:08:02,173 --> 00:08:03,944
Your turn, on your own machine.

213
00:08:04,494 --> 00:08:07,416
One: lab up, then lab files, and read both scripts.

214
00:08:07,966 --> 00:08:13,342
Two: add a secret text credential, with the ID deploy-token. Use a fake value.

215
00:08:13,892 --> 00:08:17,519
Three: build the leaky script, and find the secret in the console.

216
00:08:18,069 --> 00:08:22,080
Four: build the safe script, and check that the log shows four stars.

217
00:08:22,630 --> 00:08:25,382
Five: lab down, to remove everything you made.

218
00:08:25,932 --> 00:08:27,766
Pause the video, and begin now.

219
00:08:43,316 --> 00:08:44,191
Quick check.

220
00:08:44,741 --> 00:08:46,085
Where do secrets leak?

221
00:08:46,635 --> 00:08:49,237
What does a pipeline mention, instead of the secret?

222
00:08:49,787 --> 00:08:52,219
What does masking do, and what can it not do?

223
00:08:52,769 --> 00:08:55,948
Which rule do you break, if you echo a secret to debug?

224
00:08:56,498 --> 00:08:58,847
Objective covered: MC17,

225
00:08:58,847 --> 00:08:59,915
objective five.

226
00:08:59,915 --> 00:09:03,901
Managing credentials and environment variables securely.

227
00:09:04,451 --> 00:09:06,520
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

228
00:09:17,070 --> 00:09:19,886
Next: triggers, quality gates and publishing.

229
00:09:20,436 --> 00:09:23,764
